content.text:
It doesn’t strictly have to be a `fun`, but a plain local value would be wrong here:

```kotlin
val volumeView = HStack(...) { ... }
```

would be **constructed immediately when `body()` starts**, before the enclosing `HStack { ... }` builder scope exists. The DSL’s auto-registration works via `ViewScopes.register(...)` (`Scope.kt`): a view registers itself only if a builder scope is currently active. At `body()` top level, there is no active scope, so `volumeView` would never be added to the parent `HStack`. Referencing it later does not register it.

The local function defers construction until it is called:

```kotlin
HStack {
    ...
    if (showVolume) {
        volumeView() // constructed here, inside the parent's active scope
    }
}
```

So it registers into the correct parent, and it also avoids constructing the volume controls at all when `showVolume == false`.

Equivalent alternatives would be:

```kotlin
val volumeView: () -> View = {
    HStack(alignment = VerticalAlignment.CENTER, spacing = 18f) { ... }
}
...
volumeView()
```

